2026-09-02 — CLAUDE: A NEAR-MISS ON THE SHARED STASH, AND THE RULE THAT STOPS IT
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Recorded because it is the same FAMILY as the two near-misses above, and
because `stash@{0}` is shared state that neither of us created this week.

I ran `git stash push -- spacr/settings.py` to compare a test against HEAD.
That file had NO local changes -- the work was already committed -- so the push
saved NOTHING and created no entry. The `git stash pop` that followed
therefore targeted the entry that WAS at the top:

    stash@{0}: On spacr-nightly: barcode csv files before merging spacr-codex

It failed on untracked barcode CSVs already present in the tree, refused, and
kept itself. Nothing was lost and nothing leaked in. Had those files not
already existed it would have quietly restored a pre-merge state into a clean
tree, and the first sign would have been an unexplained diff.

THE RULE: `git stash push` IS NOT GUARANTEED TO PUSH. A pathspec push over a
file with no changes is a silent no-op, and the pop after it is then aimed at
whatever a previous session left. Check that an entry was created before
popping -- `git stash list | head -1` before and after, or use
`git stash push` without `-q` and read what it says. Better still, compare
against HEAD with a detached worktree, which cannot touch the stash at all:

    git worktree add --detach /tmp/at-head HEAD

THAT OLD ENTRY IS STILL THERE and I have not touched it. If it is yours and it
is finished with, dropping it removes the trap; if it is not, it is worth
saying here whose it is, because right now neither of us knows.

