2026-09-03 — `git commit` SWEEPS AN AGENT'S STAGED WORK. USE `-o`.
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Recorded because it happened and because the symptom is confusing rather
than loud.

`git commit` commits THE WHOLE INDEX, not the files you just added. With
several agents working in one clone, another agent's `git add` puts its work
in the same index — so a commit that names only your own files still carries
theirs, under your message. Tonight a 374 agent's nine files landed inside a
commit whose message is about instruction 371, and the agent noticed before I
did.

    git commit -o <paths> -F -        commits ONLY those paths
    git commit -F -                   commits everything staged, including
                                      whatever an agent staged a second ago

Nothing is lost when it happens -- the work is committed and correct -- but
the history says the wrong thing about who changed what and why, and that is
the whole value of these messages. `-o` costs nothing and is now the habit.

The other half of the same lesson: DO NOT `git stash` in a shared clone. It
takes every agent's uncommitted work with it, and `stash pop` after a test
run is a race with whatever they wrote meanwhile. Use a worktree for a
baseline instead — `git worktree add --detach <path> <commit>` — which is
what proved tonight that three preview tests were broken by a rename and a
fourth was not.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
2026-09-03 — TWO BACKGROUND JOBS THAT RAN FOR HOURS AND SHOULD NOT HAVE
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Both were mine and both are worth recording, because neither announced
itself: a wedged background job looks exactly like a slow one.

1. `pgrep -f "<pattern>"` MATCHES THE WAITER'S OWN COMMAND LINE.

       until ! pgrep -f "build_documentation_i18n.py --audit"; do sleep 30; done

   The shell running that loop has the pattern IN its own `bash -c` argument,
   so `pgrep -f` finds it, the condition is never false, and the loop waits
   for itself forever. It ran 43 minutes before anything noticed, and would
   have run until the session ended.

   Match on something the waiter does not contain -- a PID captured when the
   job started, a marker line in its log, or `pgrep -f` with a pattern the
   loop spells differently from the process it is watching.

2. A REPAIR PASS RAN FOR 6 HOURS 18 MINUTES AGAINST A MOVING SOURCE.

   `--repair-api-blocks` over nine locales, started 19:50, still going at
   02:08. Its useful output landed at 00:31 and was committed; nothing was
   written in the ninety minutes before it was stopped. It was chasing
   docstrings the other session was still editing -- 199 English records
   went stale again while it worked -- so finishing would not have helped.

   THE FIX IS THE ONE THE 306 PASS PROPOSED: a `--freeze-sources` snapshot,
   so a nine-language run is generated from ONE tree by construction. Until
   that exists, do not start a repair pass while another session is
   committing to `spacr/`.

   IT CACHES ON THE RESEARCH VOLUME. `--model-root` points at
   `toxoplasma_projects/.../translation_models/opus`, and the tool writes
   `.spacr_translation_cache` beside the models -- 148 MB now. That is the
   tool's design and the cache is worth keeping (it is what makes a second
   pass fast), but it is a WRITE to the 1.1 TB volume and should be a
   deliberate one. The 306 pass mirrored the models into scratch to avoid
   it; that mirror is still there and is the safer default.

