2026-09-04 — A `git add -A` SWEPT ANOTHER SESSION'S STAGED WORK. NOTHING LOST.
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

The thing section 3 warns about happened, in the direction the warning does
not describe. Section 3 says commit small and often so a CONFLICT stays small.
This was not a conflict: commit `12e9f2dc3` ("The dock's black box was the
missing backdrop") carries, besides its dock work,

    D    instructions/PROGRESS.md
    R100 instructions/open/58_... -> instructions/done/58_...

both of which were MINE and staged, waiting for their own commit and their own
message. A `git add -A` in the other session took them.

NOTHING WAS LOST AND NOTHING WAS WRONG ON DISK. What was lost is the REASON:
two deliberate decisions -- retiring a stale second ledger, and closing the
LAST item in the maintainer's ordering -- landed inside a commit about a dock
backdrop, where nobody reading the history will ever find them. `72a17f73b`
carries the reasons afterwards, which is the repair available, not the outcome
wanted.

ONE MECHANICAL DETAIL WORTH KNOWING, because it silently changes content:
`git mv` stages the rename from the INDEX, not the worktree. I had appended
58's closing note and then run `git mv`, so the swept rename carried the file
at its PRE-APPEND content and my note stayed behind as an unstaged
modification. If a sweep takes a rename you staged, check whether it took the
version you meant.

WHAT WOULD PREVENT IT, and it is already in section 3: explicit paths.

    git add -A                      <- takes whatever anyone else staged
    git commit -F - -- path/one     <- takes only what you name

Section 3's own example uses a bare `git add -A`. That is the line to change:
in a shared tree it is not a convenience, it is a way to publish someone
else's half-finished decision under your commit message. Every commit from
this session today passed explicit paths, which is why only the two ALREADY
STAGED items were reachable.

  --- 2026-09-04, Claude (dock/per-object session) ---
