  `spacr/schema.py` is free. 326 is complete and closed.

  AND I OWE AN ENTRY FOR BREAKING THE TREE. While holding schema.py I
  applied the change as TWO writes -- the references to a helper first,
  the import that defines it second -- and between them `spacr/__init__`
  could not load. About forty seconds, but the other session ran an i18n
  regression check inside that window and every test in the tree failed
  with a NameError that looked like theirs.

  THE RULE THIS SECTION SHOULD CARRY: in a shared tree, "uncommitted" is
  not the unit of care. IMPORTABLE is. An uncommitted change is normal
  here and blocks nobody; an unimportable package blocks everyone and
  produces failures attributed to whoever runs next. An edit that would
  leave the package unable to import has to be a single write, and
  checked with an actual import afterwards rather than assumed.

  Their diagnosis was exact -- half-applied edit, referenced twice,
  defined nowhere, `git status` showing modified rather than committed --
  which is why it cost minutes instead of an evening.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
2026-09-04 — A SHARED FILE NEEDS THE SAME CARE AS A SHARED TREE
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Sections 1 and 2 divide the repository by FILE, and section 3 protects the tree
with explicit paths. Both assume that if two sessions stay in their own files
they cannot collide. Today proved that wrong twice in one file, in both
directions.

WHAT I DID. Commit `2f7a33f5c` wrote `tools/build_documentation_i18n.py` from
my own buffer. The other session had removed 94 obsolete entries from
`API_DOC_ALIASES` in that same file minutes earlier; my write put all 113 back.
They did not notice for twenty minutes. Explicit paths did not help -- the path
WAS mine to write, and the collision was inside it.

WHAT THEY DID, earlier the same day: left the same file, and separately
`spacr/schema.py`, in a state that could not be imported, which blocked every
test run in the tree. That one is recorded above.

THE RULE THAT COVERS BOTH: a file is not owned just because a lane table names
it. Before writing a file another session has touched today, RE-READ IT and
apply your change to what is there now, rather than writing a buffer you formed
earlier. An editor that replaces whole-file content is a clobber even when the
path is yours, and it leaves no conflict marker to warn either side.

  * Prefer targeted edits over whole-file writes in any file the other session
    has touched today.
  * If you generated content into a buffer minutes ago, re-read before writing.
  * `git status` on the single file before you write it costs nothing and would
    have caught both incidents.

AND THE HONEST VERSION OF WHY THIS HAPPENED TO ME: I was applying a scripted
edit that read the file, transformed it in memory, and wrote it back. Between
the read and the write, somebody else's work landed. That is the same shape as
"a baseline taken at the wrong moment is not a baseline", which I had already
recorded about test runs today and did not think to apply to file writes.

