--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
2026-09-05, LATER — THE ALIAS REGISTRY WAS NOT A MYSTERY, AND IS NOW GREEN
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

The 2026-09-04 entry called `API_DOC_ALIASES` being empty against a test that
wants 113 "a question for whoever owns the alias registry", and declined to
guess. Right call, and the answer turned out to be written down already.

`67312bb19` — "368: the public half is DONE. Zero undocumented public methods,
zero aliases" — emptied it ON PURPOSE, and the comment above the definition
explains why: an alias let an UNDOCUMENTED public override borrow its base
class's text, so once every public method had its own docstring all 113 entries
became lies, hiding the specific answer behind generic text. So the CODE was
right and the TEST was stale. Not a guess: their own commit message and their
own comment.

Both alias tests are green:
  * `test_exact_alias_map_...` pinned `len == 113` and a digest of the old
    entries. It now pins the end state, and KEEPS the structural invariants
    rather than deleting them -- they cost nothing on an empty map and are
    exactly what a future entry would need checking against. Renamed to say
    what it means: an alias is a SIGNAL that a public override lacks a
    docstring, and the fix is the docstring.
  * `test_localized_manifest_materializes_alias_translation` tests the
    MECHANISM, which must keep working for the day an alias is legitimately
    added. It now injects the registry entry and sets up the precondition it
    used to get free -- an alias is only valid when the two texts are
    identical, which is what an alias means and what `write_language`
    enforces.

STILL RED, AND YOURS TO RE-RECORD WHEN 368 LANDS — four count ratchets that
move every time you document another symbol, so re-recording them now would be
stale within the hour and would only fight your commits:

    test_documentation_api_catalog_inventory_and_hashes_are_current  10190 vs 10152
    test_public_docstrings_matches_reviewed_visible_coverage         177 vs 69
    test_assignment_docs_are_ast_source_text_without_show_value...   16 vs 18
    test_public_docstrings_exclude_the_exact_non_rendered_autoapi_boundary

I have deliberately not touched them. Ten doc-gate failures, six now closed.

