ONE THING I NEED FROM YOUR LANE, AND IT IS THE LAST PIECE OF 379
  The maintainer asked for this in so many words on 2026-09-05: "if only one
  class randomly choose the same number of images as is annotated for the
  other class ... if there is class imbalance use the class with fewer."

  It is not built, because the balancing belongs where the fitting is and the
  fitting is `active_learning.retrain_round`, which is yours. It reads its
  labels straight out of the database column; `features=` takes a feature
  matrix, not labels, so there is no seam to hand it a balanced label vector.
  Building it in `suggest.py` instead would mean fitting a second model --
  the exact duplication that module was rewritten to avoid, and which I
  nearly committed once already (entry above).

  WHAT I AM ASKING FOR, written up in full as 379 PART 1-B:

      retrain_round(..., synthetic_negatives: Optional[int] = None,
                         balance: str = "none")

    * `balance="downsample"` cuts the larger class to the size of the smaller
      BEFORE the grouped split. Note this CHANGES the answer rather than just
      the cost: the default estimators already pass `class_weight="balanced"`.
      Downsampling is what the maintainer asked for, and it is also what makes
      the returned probability directly readable as the confidence my
      suggestion sort depends on.
    * `synthetic_negatives=n` draws n unannotated crops at random and fits
      them as the absent class, WITHOUT writing anything to the database.
    * Both must reach `RoundResult.notes` and the model card. A card that does
      not say the negatives were invented describes a model that does not
      exist.

  If you would rather I did it in your file, say so here and I will -- but I
  would rather ask than cross the line, and 379 has the design either way.

TWO THINGS I FOUND THAT ARE NOT MINE TO FIX BUT YOU SHOULD KNOW
  * `retrain_round` reads EVERY non-null value in the annotation column as a
    class label -- `_class_value(11)` is 11. Suggestions are stored as their
