2026-09-07, WORK SESSION — A TEST THAT CHANGES A GLOBAL OWNS PUTTING IT BACK
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Three instances in three days, from both sessions, and the third is what made
the shape obvious enough to write down.

    QSettings         a test called `prefs.set_ambient_enabled(False)` and
                      never restored it.
    _INITIALISED      `logging_util.setup_logging` returned early on a flag
                      that no longer described the world; my attempt to make
                      it re-verify traded a flake for a certainty and was
                      reverted.
    propagate         `cli.setup_logging` sets `LOG.propagate = False` and
                      installs its own stdout handler -- correct for a batch
                      run -- and `test_setup_logging_replaces_its_handler`
                      made that change on behalf of the entire session.

WHY IT IS EXPENSIVE IS NOT THE BUG. It is WHERE THE COST LANDS. None of these
broke the test that made the change; all three broke a DIFFERENT file, later,
which is why each read as an unrelated flake and got bisected rather than
recognised. The propagate one reported an empty `caplog.text` for a message
the code emits correctly and prints to stderr -- the product was right and the
harness had been blinded by a test that had already finished.

AND IT REACHES THE DEVELOPER'S OWN INSTALL. The QSettings one turned the
maintainer's ambient animation OFF in the installed application; he reported
it as a product defect and an hour went into bisecting the wrong thing. A
test's process-global write does not stop at the process when the store is on
disk.

THE RULE, and it is cheap to follow:

  * A test that changes process-global state -- QSettings, environment,
    logging configuration, a module-level flag, sys.modules, a registry --
    restores it in a `finally` or a fixture, even when the change looks
    harmless and especially when it is CORRECT for production.
  * "Correct for production" is what disguises all three of these. Nobody
    doubts a CLI should not print twice, or that `setup_logging` should be
    idempotent. The fault is the SCOPE of the decision, not the decision.
  * When a test fails for a reason that has nothing to do with its subject,
    look for a global before looking for a cause. Passing alone and failing in
    a suite is the signature.

RELATED, and the same family one level up: a fake that predates a new seam.
Both backdrop fakes lacked the `window_backdrop`/`window()` the one-per-window
guard now asks about, so every arc below the guard asserted the behaviour of a
window that had already been served. Not a global, but the same reading -- the
test was answering a question the code had stopped asking.

