THE PAIR I AM HANDING OVER, because it is a routing decision in shared
logging code and I am not making one at five in the morning:

    tests/qt/test_a_qt_warning_reaches_the_console_once.py
      ::test_a_qt_warning_is_rendered_once_not_twice
    tests/qt/test_a_small_machine_gets_a_smaller_backdrop.py
      ::test_applying_it_reports_what_it_actually_wrote

BOTH PASS ALONE. Bisected to a two-file reproduction:

    pytest tests/qt/test_app_screen.py \
           tests/qt/test_a_qt_warning_reaches_the_console_once.py

`test_app_screen.py` only builds an `AppScreen`. That constructor registers a
console target and calls `verbose_logger._ensure_handler()`, and from then on
one Qt warning is rendered TWICE -- once through `logging_util`'s signal
handler ("root") and once through the verbose forwarder ("verbose"). The test
collects from both sinks deliberately and asserts one line, which is what its
name says.

  SO THIS MAY NOT BE A TEST-ISOLATION BUG AT ALL. If the double render is what
  the application does once a console exists, then in a running spaCR every Qt
  warning appears twice in the console -- and the test is right rather than
  fragile. That is the thing to establish first, and it decides the fix:
  either the sinks stop overlapping, or the test stops counting both.

  WHAT I DID NOT DO, deliberately: change the routing between
  `spacr/qt/verbose_logger.py` and `spacr/logging_util.py`. Both are shared,
  the fan-out is intentional ("Every ConsolePanel subscribes to the same
  shared signal handler, so log records fanned out across screens all see
  them"), and picking one owner for Qt warnings is a decision rather than a
  repair.

