THE SECOND ORDER-DEPENDENT PAIR, handed over rather than fixed:

    pytest tests/qt/test_scrim_solver.py \
           tests/qt/test_settings_column_never_black.py

  -> 24 failed. Each file passes alone. Bisected to exactly those two.

  The victim's message is "0.0% of the settings column is unpainted and 47.1%
  is the window colour", i.e. the page is painting `bg` instead of the page
  colour -- the black-box family again.

  WHAT MAKES IT INTERESTING: `test_scrim_solver.py` only uses `monkeypatch`
  (on `get_ambient_enabled`, `resolve_effective_theme`,
  `theme_background_path`), and pytest reverts those. So the leak is NOT the
  patch -- it is STATE COMPUTED WHILE THE PATCH WAS ACTIVE AND KEPT
  AFTERWARDS. A QApplication-level stylesheet applied under the fake theme is
  the obvious candidate and I did not confirm it; `theme.py`'s two
  `lru_cache`s are NOT it -- both are pure functions of their arguments.

  WHY I STOPPED HERE rather than pushing on: this is the sixth global-state
  leak this week and the subtlest, and I had already made two errors tonight
  from confident reading of code I had not measured. Handing over a
  reproduction I trust beats a fix I do not.

  IT ALSO EXTENDS THE RULE WE HAVE BOTH BEEN WRITING DOWN. "A test that
  changes process-global state owns putting it back" is not enough when the
  mutation is reverted correctly and something DERIVED from it is cached. The
  question to ask is not "did I restore it" but "did anything read it while it
  was wrong, and keep the answer".

  MEASURED 2026-09-08, and it killed two hypotheses including my own:

    * MY FIRST PROBE WAS THE BUG. Hooked on `pytest_runtest_teardown`, it
      reported "the scrim file leaves the persisted theme as `cell`". It does
      not. That hook runs BEFORE monkeypatch's finalizer, so the probe was
      reading the patch. `pytest_runtest_setup` is the honest place to stand:
      it is after the previous test's teardown has completed. Anyone
      measuring test leakage should hook SETUP, not teardown.

      WHY THIS ONE IS WORSE THAN AN ORDINARY WRONG ANSWER, and worth the
      space: a probe on teardown does not fail sometimes. It reports a leak
      on EVERY correctly-restored test, because every correctly-restored
      test still has its patch in place at that moment. It is a
      false-positive generator aimed precisely at the class of bug both
      sessions have spent the week hunting -- so its output looks like the
      thing you are already looking for, arrives in volume, and is entirely
      spurious. An instrument that agrees with your hypothesis on every
      sample is not evidence, and the cost of believing it is a day.

      THE TEST FOR ANY LEAK PROBE, then: run it against a file you are
      confident is clean. If it still reports a leak, the probe is the bug.
      That control costs one run and is the only thing that separates this
      from a real finding.
    * NOT the `_WIDGET_QSS` one-way shrink that
      `_widget_qss_registrars_loaded` exists to prevent, which was the
      obvious suspect because the conftest documents it as THE black-box
      mechanism. Watched across both files, the registry goes 4 -> 42 and
      stays at 42, and the built stylesheet length never moves.

  WHAT IS REAL: the QApplication stylesheet ends at ZERO BYTES and `style`
  bounces `fusion -> "" -> fusion`. `test_scrim_solver` itself restores what
  it sets, and `qt_theme_applied` only ever applies -- it has no teardown --
  so something between those tests is putting back an EMPTY snapshot. That
  is the same one-way pattern as the QSS registry, one level up. The fixture
  doing it is not found; the work session has the conftest context.

