2026-09-08 — REBUILD THE API CATALOGS ONCE, AT THE SHIP BOUNDARY, NOT PER CHANGE
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

AGREED BY BOTH SESSIONS, with the work session's condition attached and met.

THE MEASUREMENT THAT SETTLED IT. `--repair-api-blocks` only writes WHOLE
LOCALES, so the cost of a rebuild is a function of the number of LOCALES and
not of the number of changed symbols. Measured today: ten text-changed
docstrings cost one nine-locale rebuild, and then ONE text-changed docstring
-- `training_monitor.TrainingMonitor`, from removing an `Attributes` section
that `class_content='both'` was concatenating into `__init__` -- cost a second
identical one. Every rebuild before the last is speculative work.

THE RULE. The API catalogs are allowed to be STALE during ordinary work. The
work session changes public docstrings freely and does not need to announce
them. The home session rebuilds ONCE, last, immediately before the version is
cut, and that rebuild is a named line in 331 rather than a thing either of us
remembers.

THE CONDITION, which is the work session's and is the reason the rule is safe:

    "a known-red test that is allowed to stay red is exactly the artefact
    that keeps looking like it is doing its job"

So the rule is void unless 331 carries the rebuild as a checklist line, and
unless the test says why it is red in its own output. Both are now true:
331 has the step with the per-locale command and the GPU-OOM caveat, and
`test_documentation_api_catalog_inventory_and_hashes_are_current` is xfailed
with a reason naming 325 and 331. A reader who hits it learns where to look
instead of asking one of us.

WHAT REPLACED THE OLD HANDSHAKE. Earlier the same day the home session asked
for a heads-up before any public docstring change, and the work session
agreed and stopped. That was the wrong trade: it made one lane wait on the
other to buy a rebuild that was going to be thrown away. This rule costs
nobody any waiting.

