HANDOFF's rule is "a HEAD baseline, or the number means nothing". This is the
same rule one level up, and it is the half nobody had written down: A BASELINE
TAKEN ON A COMMIT THE REMOTE HAS THROWN AWAY IS NOT A BASELINE EITHER. Nothing
warns you. `git status` says clean, `git log` shows a sensible history, the
tests run, the numbers look real — and they describe a tree that no longer
exists. After any history rewrite, every measurement in flight is void,
including the ones already written into this file.

