--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
2026-09-09 — A TEST RUN IS A READER OF THE WORKING TREE, AND THE TREE IS NOT
YOURS TO MOVE WHILE IT HAS ONE
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Recorded by repo-01 on the other session's report, and the suggestion that
caused it was MINE, which is the part worth saying first.

I told them their HANDOFF pointer fix was sitting unpushed and was "worth
pushing on its own, ahead of the catalog work". To push one file alone they
ran

    git stash push spacr/qt/screens/map_barcodes.py

to hold the code change back — AND THAT REVERTED THEIR 382 FIX IN THE WORKING
TREE WHILE A FULL tests/qt RUN WAS EXECUTING AGAINST IT. Restored one tool
call later, so the window was seconds. Seconds is enough: pytest imports at
COLLECTION, so any fold test collected inside that window would have been
testing the unfixed file and reporting a failure against code that was
already correct — a failure they would then have spent an hour chasing in the
fix rather than in the tree.

THIS IS NOT THE STASH RULE ALREADY IN THIS FILE. Two entries above cover
stash: that `git stash push -u` takes every other agent's uncommitted work,
and that a PATHSPEC push over an unchanged file is a silent no-op whose pop
then aims at somebody else's entry. Both are about the stash being WRONG.
This one is about a stash that is entirely CORRECT — narrowly scoped, it
saved what it said, it restored what it saved, no other session's work was
anywhere near it — and was still destructive, because the tree had a READER.

THE RULE. While a test run is in flight, the working tree is not yours to
move, and that includes the operations that feel safe because they only touch
files you are not testing:

    git stash push -- <path>          reverts <path> for the duration
    git pull --rebase --autostash     same thing, implicitly, twice
    git rebase                        rewrites the tree under the reader
    git checkout <commit> -- <path>   same, narrowed, still a write

THE REPOSITORY IS SHARED WITH A PROCESS, NOT ONLY WITH ANOTHER SESSION.
Sections 1 and 2 divide the tree between two sessions and a later entry
extends that to a shared FILE; this extends it again, to a shared MOMENT. An
edit is safe against a person who will read the file later and unsafe against
a process reading it now, and `git status` cannot tell you which you have.

WHAT TO DO INSTEAD, and it is already the answer to two other entries here:
do the comparison, the baseline or the partial push from a DETACHED WORKTREE,
which has its own checkout and cannot move the tree a test is reading.

    git worktree add --detach /tmp/wt origin/nightly

Both entries of mine in this file were written from one, which is the only
reason a session with no claim on anything could measure a shared tree at all
while three others committed to it.

AND THE ADVICE HALF, since I gave it: "push that one file on its own" is
cheap to say and is not free to do. It is only cheap when the tree is idle.
The right sequencing was theirs — let the qt run finish, then one push
carrying the pointers, the 372 PART 8 append and the map_barcodes fix
together — because CI cannot conclude on this branch, so a local run is the
ONLY evidence 382 is going to get, and corrupting it to save seven minutes
would have cost the evidence outright.
