WORK SESSION — TWO THINGS FOR YOU, BOTH IN YOUR FILES AND NEITHER TOUCHED.

1. `ops_merge.align_by_triangles` HAS A SCALE BIAS AND NO WAY TO SAY NO.
   Found while building Phase A4 on the real plate, measured on 593 real
   nuclei with the transform known exactly:

       what was asked for              scale returned    inliers
       identity                            0.9878          56 %
       scale 0.25                          0.2469         100 %
       scale 0.25, 1 px of jitter          0.2065          53 %
       scale 0.25, half the points kept    0.2916          23 %
       scale 0.25, a third kept            0.3596          15 %

   A 1.2 % scale error is 18 px across a 1480 px field, which is why an
   EXACT copy of a point set scores 56 rather than 100. And it returned a
   transform for every pair it was ever handed, including phenotype and
   sequencing tiles from opposite sides of the well -- three coincidentally
   similar triangles exist in any two sets of a few hundred points.

   I did NOT edit your module. `spacr/ops_phenotype.py` takes its consensus
   as a SEED and re-solves the similarity from the correspondences that seed
   implies -- which is the argument your own docstring already makes for the
   translation, applied to the scale and rotation as well -- and reports an
   inlier count so a caller can tell an alignment from a coincidence. If you
   would rather that refinement lived in `ops_merge` beside the consensus,
   say so here and I will move it and delete mine.

2. THE FIX THAT MATTERS MOST IS NOT IN EITHER MODULE: the scale is not
   something A4 has to estimate. `round_well_layout(1281)` fits the
   phenotype acquisition at 41 columns and radius 20.15 against the
   sequencing well's 21 at 10.25 -- the same round well at twice the tile
   density, 41 = 2*21 - 1 -- so the magnification ratio is 0.2543 in closed
   form, and the plate does not rotate between acquisitions. Bounding scale
   and rotation by those two facts is what turned this from noise into a
   result: phenotype site 41 lands on sequencing site 23 at 132 agreeing
   nuclei against a chance floor of 6, scale 0.2510, rotation -0.03 deg,
   residual 1.58 px.

