HOME SESSION — 2026-09-10, A RED TEST IN `ops_accel`, DIAGNOSED AND NOT YET
FIXED. `torch.cdist`'s DEFAULT compute_mode.

`tests/test_the_ops_primitives_agree_on_every_backend.py::
test_chunking_changes_nothing_but_the_working_set` fails, deterministically
-- the fixture is seeded with 9 -- on current nightly. It is not flaky and it
is not mine: nothing I have touched is in that path.

    whole = nearest_neighbours(source, target, chunk=len(source))
    pieces = nearest_neighbours(source, target, chunk=7)
    np.array_equal(whole[0], pieces[0])      # indices agree
    np.allclose(whole[1], pieces[1])         # DISTANCES DO NOT

THE CAUSE IS `torch.cdist`'s DEFAULT `compute_mode`, which is
`use_mm_for_euclid_dist_if_necessary`: it switches to the
||a||^2 + ||b||^2 - 2ab matrix-multiply expansion when the batch is big
enough, and takes the direct route when it is not. So a chunk of 500 and a
chunk of 7 run different arithmetic, and the expansion's cancellation error
is what comes out. Measured on the test's own fixture:

    compute_mode                        chunk-independent   max err vs exact
    (default)                                 no                4.14e-03
    use_mm_for_euclid_dist                    no                4.14e-03
    donot_use_mm_for_euclid_dist             YES                4.77e-07

THE FIX IS ONE KEYWORD, and it is `donot_use_mm_for_euclid_dist` rather than
forcing mm the other way: it is chunk-independent AND four orders of
magnitude more accurate, and it matches the numpy path, which is what
`ops_accel`'s own docstring promises -- "every one has a test that runs BOTH
paths on the same input and asserts they agree".

It matters beyond the test. `ops_merge.match_cells` thresholds these
distances, so a 4e-3 error that moves with the working-set size is a pairing
that changes when the well gets bigger.

I HAVE NOT MADE THE EDIT. `ops_accel.py` is yours under this file, and a
nine-language catalog rebuild is running against the working tree here --
touching a module mid-rebuild is how I lost four locales on 2026-09-09.
Take it if you are awake; otherwise I will apply exactly the one keyword
after the rebuild lands and say so here.

HOME SESSION — 2026-09-10, THE `ops_accel` RED TEST IS FIXED. ONE KEYWORD.

Reported the diagnosis here a few hours ago and said I would apply it after
the catalog rebuild landed rather than touch your file mid-rebuild. Done, in
24dc26f10:

    torch.cdist(block, target_tensor,
                compute_mode="donot_use_mm_for_euclid_dist")

`test_chunking_changes_nothing_but_the_working_set` passes, and 769 tests
across the OPS files pass with it. The comment beside the call carries the
measurement so nobody removes the keyword as noise.

If you would rather it were done another way, it is one line and I will
revert it -- but the default cannot stay: `ops_merge.match_cells` thresholds
those distances, so an error that moves with the working-set size is a
pairing that changes when the well gets bigger, and that is the kind of
defect that shows up as a barcode that decodes differently on a big plate.

ALSO IN YOUR FILES, AND ONLY THE RST: `ops_layout.py` and `ops_register.py`
each had a docstring block whose lead-in ended in a single colon with a
continuation line indented deeper underneath. docutils reads that as a
blockquote and errors, and `docs.yml` runs `sphinx-build -W`, so those two
docstrings have been failing the whole documentation job. Both now end the
lead-in with `::`, which makes the block literal; RST renders a trailing
double colon as a single one, so the page is identical. `sphinx-build -W`
exits 0 now -- for the first time since 2026-09-08.

`tests/test_every_docstring_is_valid_rst.py` catches the next one in 28
seconds and names the FUNCTION, instead of a twenty-minute build naming a
generated file.

